    <header>
      <h1>About the project</h1>
      <p>A web development research project by Deborah Daramola, built around the evolution of HTTP and HTTPS.</p>
    </header>
    
      
        <section><h2>About the author</h2><p>I am Deborah Daramola, and I created this website as a research project for IT 3203. My topic explores the evolution of HTTP and HTTPS and the relationship between web performance, communication, and security.</p><p>This project gives me a way to practice presenting technical information through a clear, accessible website. The content builds on my Assignment 1 research and connects protocol concepts to familiar browsing tasks.</p></section>
        <section><h2>Project focus</h2><p>The site explains why HTTP changed, how HTTPS protects communication, and which concepts remain useful across versions. It aims to help a beginning web development student understand the connection between a browser request and the technologies carrying it.</p><h3>Skills demonstrated</h3><ul><li>Semantic HTML and a consistent structure across six pages.</li><li>One shared CSS stylesheet with element, class, and ID selectors.</li><li>CSS Grid and Flexbox, with readable navigation and page layouts.</li><li>Accessible images, a comparison table, and linked research citations.</li></ul></section>
        <section><h2>Learning objectives</h2><ol><li>Explain the differences among major HTTP versions.</li><li>Distinguish secure transport from broader website trust.</li><li>Connect requests, responses, and status codes to real browser activity.</li><li>Present research with clear navigation and reliable references.</li></ol></section>
        <section><h2>Milestone roadmap</h2><p>Milestone 1 establishes this informational HTML/CSS site. Later milestones will add a JavaScript knowledge quiz and develop the final responsive experience and research. This version includes basic flexible layouts so the content remains readable.</p><p class="source-note">This website is an educational project, not a commercial product or security assessment.</p></section>
      
    
